# SYNOPSIS

```usage
chgrp [-h] [-R [-H | -L | -P]] group file ...
```

# DESCRIPTION

`chgrp` sets the group of each <file> to <group>, a group's name or number.
A name is looked up in `/etc/group`; what is not found there must be a number.
The owner is left alone.

: `-h`
  Change a symbolic link named as a <file> itself, not what it points to.
: `-R`
  Change directories' contents too, recursively.
: `-H`, `-L`, `-P`
  With `-R`: follow symbolic links named as a <file> only; follow every symbolic link; or follow none, the default.

`chgrp` is sbase's, a hard link to `/boot/bin/posix/sbase-box`; there is no native one.
In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so a POSIX program finds it as `/bin/chgrp`.

# SEE ALSO

chown(1), chmod(1), users(6); sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/chgrp.1` in the repository

# DIAGNOSTICS

Fewer than two arguments or an unknown flag prints the usage message and exits 1.
A <group> that is neither a known name nor a number is reported, as `chgrp: strtonum staff: invalid`, and it exits 1.
A <file> that cannot be changed is reported, as `chgrp: chown /x: Operation not permitted`, and the rest are done; the exit status is then 1.

# BUGS

There is no `/etc/group`: VectraOS keeps its users and groups in fsd(4)'s `adm/users` file (see users(6)), which the C library does not read.
Worse, by a reading of the C library, the failed open of `/etc/group` leaves `errno` set, which `chgrp` takes as the lookup failing, so even a numeric <group> is likely refused as `chgrp: getgrnam 0: No such file or directory`.
This is untested on VectraOS.

Whether a file server lets the group be changed is its own affair; tmpfs(4) and fsd(4) are untested with `chgrp`.

A failure is reported as `chown`, the call that failed, not `chgrp`.
